00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:04.360
Chris: Conclusions from the use
of coding principles and practices

00:00:05.230 --> 00:00:10.750
Following coding principles will help developer
teams to reduce dependencies, and make designs

00:00:10.750 --> 00:00:13.699
easier to understand, maintain and extend.

00:00:14.420 --> 00:00:20.919
This helps developers to build adaptive, effective software
which would also be in line with Agile methodology.

00:00:21.780 --> 00:00:27.979
However, following coding principles can extend the
design process and make development more difficult, and

00:00:27.980 --> 00:00:31.130
it can be seen as a tedious task by some developers.

00:00:32.170 --> 00:00:37.960
Leah: As the intention of the principles is to make
code more readable, easier to understand, easier to

00:00:37.960 --> 00:00:45.420
test and maintain; most developer teams will consider
the effort and extra time to implement them a valuable

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:51.800
exercise, as this will help to avoid issues that
could occur from not following them at a later date.

00:00:52.309 --> 00:00:58.989
So let’s look at some of the principles we have already
discussed and how they can affect code starting with KISS.

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:03.980
Chris: As we discussed in the previous video
the main rule with KISS is that the code

00:01:03.980 --> 00:01:06.690
should be simple and therefore easy to modify.

00:01:07.370 --> 00:01:12.449
By keeping the programming simple, service and
maintenance are much easier, problems are solved

00:01:12.469 --> 00:01:20.449
quicker, the code is deliberately concise, and it
is easier to understand, extend, change, and reuse.

00:01:21.110 --> 00:01:23.069
Now let’s consider SOLID

00:01:24.300 --> 00:01:29.600
Leah: S - The Single Responsibility
Principle [SRP] title on screen

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:35.840
Good practice of SRP makes code easier to test and
maintain and will prevent side-effects from future

00:01:35.840 --> 00:01:39.080
changes, making the software easier to implement.

00:01:39.320 --> 00:01:42.819
Chris: O – Open / Closed Principle [OCP] title on screen

00:01:42.819 --> 00:01:49.020
Good practice of OCP makes code that is easy
to maintain and revise as you can add new

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.929
functionality without changing the existing code.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:58.139
This is useful in agile methodologies as
stakeholders’ requirements are likely to change.

00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:03.230
Leah: L – Liskov Substitution
Principle [LSP] title on screen

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:10.750
Good practice of LSP helps to avoid unexpected
consequences of changes, avoids having to open a closed

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:17.859
class, avoids issues that could occur during updates
and extensions, and allows easy extensions of software.

00:02:19.059 --> 00:02:23.899
Chris: I – Interface Segregation
Principle [ISP] title on screen

00:02:23.899 --> 00:02:28.730
With ISP as the developers would be focused on
their own client specific interface, they are not

00:02:28.730 --> 00:02:32.410
forced to depend on interfaces that they do not use.

00:02:32.870 --> 00:02:37.120
This would save the developer time and
increase their motivation and focus.

00:02:38.389 --> 00:02:41.969
Leah: D – Dependency Inversion
Principle [DIP] title on screen

00:02:42.680 --> 00:02:49.820
As high-level modules [which sustain complex systems] are
not affected by low-level modules [which provide utility

00:02:49.820 --> 00:02:57.589
features] with DIP; this premise would speed up development
and make it easier to develop and update the code.

00:02:58.230 --> 00:03:04.365
Chris: So, in conclusion using good coding practices
and principles can help create better and more organised

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:12.589
workflow, increased efficiency and productivity,
enhanced product quality and subsequently reduced costs.

00:03:13.259 --> 00:03:17.989
Also using coding practices and principles to
avoid the frustration that comes with bugs and

00:03:17.990 --> 00:03:20.960
errors should be motivation enough in itself.

